      "math_academy_id": "q-125692",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/98e64a245737736bfc987fe9230b1ee3fc81eef4aedfea8c2e5e9bcf949f8203.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-\\frac{2}{3}x,-\\frac{2}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(-3,-3),$ $(0,0),$  and $(-3,3),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3,-3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3))=(2,2) \\\\ (0,0) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0)=(0,0) \\\\ (-3,3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3)=(2,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/2921272ffd1c38c082bf2123086d8c7b19a910c91d132810042b86728b820f8d.png)",
